# Release signing trust root for agent-sessions (#832).
#
# OpenSSH allowed_signers format, read verbatim by:
#   git -c gpg.ssh.allowedSignersFile=<this file> verify-tag <tag>
#
# ONE entry covers every FUTURE release — the property a per-release manifest
# structurally cannot provide, because a build can only ever carry entries for
# releases older than itself. This key outlives the releases it vouches for.
#
# Public key material only. Adding a line grants release authority; removing one
# revokes it for every build shipped afterwards. Custody, rotation and recovery:
# docs/release-signing.md (export-ignored — it names hosts and paths).
# The primary key can sign any release by hand. The recovery key (same principal, so every check that
# names release@agent-sessions holds for either) signs NOTHING in normal operation: it exists
# to sign the bridge release that introduces a new primary if the primary or its passphrase is
# lost — the one case the rotation bridge cannot cover, because that bridge needs the old key.
# The automation key (#2379) signs routine releases unattended, from an agent session; the
# operator asked for releases that need no hand on them. It is the third line because it was
# added last, not because it ranks last: any line here is a full signer.
release@agent-sessions ssh-ed25519 AAAAC3NzaC1lZDI1NTE5AAAAIOjW+gor5BTHMCjx6GWhCOJXdmR9Lei9elkzV7j++zbX agent-sessions release signing
release@agent-sessions ssh-ed25519 AAAAC3NzaC1lZDI1NTE5AAAAIE3LUURaBPIGDEAluzrmDCS++MwgQ0DHL23J+3TjcLKL agent-sessions release recovery
release@agent-sessions ssh-ed25519 AAAAC3NzaC1lZDI1NTE5AAAAIA//f+wPEejdHCgLUtD6uBV1sOwBXnT+rdeCW38rcXEy agent-sessions release automation
